.TH git-upstream-pull "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset 0.156.0" "SMI Git Command"
.SH NAME
git-upstream-pull \- merge the changes of the template repository into the current branch
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git upstream-pull
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBgit upstream-pull\fR command is for a private project made from a template by
\fBsmi-project-prepare\fR(1), where the template is the remote \fBupstream\fR. It brings the later
changes of the template into the project:
.IP 1. 4
\fBgit fetch \-\-prune upstream\fR.
.IP 2. 4
\fBgit merge \-\-no\-edit upstream/master\fR into the checked out branch, with the default merge message.
.PP
On a merge conflict Git stops as with any merge: resolve the files, then \fBgit add\fR and
\fBgit commit\fR, or \fBgit merge \-\-abort\fR. Nothing is pushed, \fBgit push\fR or \fBgit save\fR
publishes the merge to \fBorigin\fR afterwards.
.PP
The project cleanup of \fBsmi-project-cleanup\fR(1) is part of the project history, so files removed
there stay removed. A file the template changed after the cleanup removed it gives a modify/delete
conflict: \fBgit rm\fR it to keep it removed.
.PP
Pushing to the template is not done: its push URL is \fBDISABLED\fR, so the private code can not reach
it by accident.
.PP
Git runs it as a subcommand because the executable \fBgit-upstream-pull\fR is in the \fBPATH\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Merge the template changes into develop and publish them:
.PP
.nf
.B git checkout develop
.B git upstream-pull
.B git push
.fi
